What this solution actually does: it is the inbound parser/validator for the liquidation instructions that Calypso writes into the TOMI interchange table. On every timer tick it marks the start of the tick in TOMI, reads the TOMI system date and two parameters (HORA_OP_INICIO, HORA_OP_CEP_FIN), refuses to run on a non-business day (raising an alert) or outside the operating window, and then asks usp_CLiquidacion_GetSolicitudesLiquidacion for every row of tbl_calypso_intercambio with estatus = 'RECV' and tipo = 'LIQ' (id_envio, msg_out). Each message is matched, in fixed order, against seven regular expressions: three CSV layouts (T24, SPEI, SPID) and four SWIFT FIN texts (MT103, MT202, MT210, MT300); the first match wins and its named groups become a LiquidacionModel. Validation then normalises the amount, derives the direction (E for receive, S for pay, always S for the four SWIFT types), and checks T24: for T24 the account must exist in the T24 view, its currency must equal the message currency and its customer must equal the CDL; for SPEI/SPID and the SWIFT types only the CDL customer must exist; MT300 is accepted without any lookup. The result is turned into a <row> XML and persisted by usp_CLiquidacion_InsLiquidacionCompleto, one transaction that inserts the liquidation row (tipo_operacion = MD_<type>[_<E|S>], or MD_UNKNOW when invalid), creates or completes the Entrada/Salida pair in tbl_calypso_liquidacion_par for SPEI/SPID/T24/MT103/MT202, and flips the interchange row to PROC. Rejected messages are persisted too (operacion_valida = 0, razon_rechazo), so every request leaves an audit row. The service neither moves money nor talks to Calypso; whoever consumes tbl_calypso_liquidacion (status INI) is not in this repository.
