Issue: MT300 is evaluated last for every message that matched nothing else. Its filler groups are unbounded * over a class that includes `:` and line breaks, so each :20:/:30T:/:32B: marker can be found in many places and the engine tries every combination when the tail does not match. Executed on .NET Framework 4.8 (the service runtime) with a message that has a valid MT300 header, repeated :20:/:30T:/:32B:/:33B: blocks and no -} terminator:
Repeated blocks	Message length	Result
1	123 chars	no match, 1 ms
5	335 chars	no match, 296 ms
10	600 chars	no match, 1,895 ms
20 / 30 / 40	1.1 / 1.7 / 2.2 KB	no result after 20 s (probe timeout)

A well-formed MT300 matched in 0-12 ms and a well-formed message with a trailing CRLF failed fast (13-20 ms), so the blow-up needs repeated field markers plus a missing or damaged tail: unlikely for a clean feed, but one such message holds the tick for hours, the shared WindowsServiceHostBase tick lock blocks every later tick, and a service stop waits on it. The other six patterns have bounded groups and did not blow up in the same probe (1.6 KB of junk after a valid MT202/MT210 header failed in 0 ms).
Fix: (1) give every pattern a match timeout and treat a timeout as a rejection; (2) replace the four monolithic SWIFT patterns with a tag parser, which also makes CL-FR-8/13 testable.
