Issue: the patterns accept far more than a number: CSV [0-9,.]{1,25} matches ., ,, 1,2,3 and 1.234,56; SWIFT [0-9\,]{0,15} matches 1,000,50 and the empty string. After the string Replace calls, 1.234,56 becomes 1.23456 (a 1,000x error stored without complaint), 1,000,50 becomes 1.000.50, which TRY_CAST turns into NULL, and the row is still inserted with operacion_valida = 1 and its pair record. The same normalisation is repeated inside the stored procedure (two implementations of one rule), the amount travels as string through the model and DTO, and a zero amount is accepted. (The empty-amount case also raises a NullReferenceException, see CL-FR-6.)
Fix: parse once, in the application, with an explicit format per message family; reject anything else with a business reason; pass the invariant text (or a decimal parameter) to the procedure and let the procedure THROW when the cast fails.
