5. Architecture-Fit Note
Applying the standing question in section 6 of the control document (./_ORCHESTRATION.md): this service is a timer-driven polling consumer over a table used as a queue (tbl_calypso_intercambio, RECV -> PROC), whose work per message is a single short transform-validate-persist step with the transaction and the E/S pairing inside one stored procedure. That is closer to the "message-driven" row of the trade-off table than to a "workflow/saga engine": the genuinely multi-step part (pair completion and whatever posts the liquidation in T24) lives downstream and is not in this repository, so there is nothing here to justify a saga engine or microservice split. The recommended sequence is the portfolio one: (1) fix the defects that lose, duplicate or hide instructions (CL-FR-1, 2, 4, 5, 17, 7, 28), bound the work per tick (CL-FR-3) and align identity/build (CL-FR-21, 22); (2) extract the parsers and rules, then cover them with golden-message tests (CL-FR-15, 26, 25); (3) only then decide on hosting: modernise in place (.NET 8 worker, same layering) or move the intake to a queue. The decision needs facts this audit cannot see: message volume and peak rate, the real timer interval (held in the encrypted configuration), the latency Calypso/operations expect between RECV and a usable row, hosting constraints, and who consumes tbl_calypso_liquidacion. A full rewrite is not recommended from a code-quality review alone.
